# Gitea 1.27.3 (image gitea/gitea:1.27) environment schema
# (@env-spec / varlock format).
#
# The compose pins the FLOATING minor tag `gitea/gitea:1.27`. Resolved on
# 2026-09-03 (research date): `1.27` and `latest` both carry digest
# sha256:87a67ee09d3ae0d1df5fda5dcda3e2a1f9236a45b0a59025d6e00e46adc43bef,
# the same image as `1.27.3`, the newest stable release (2026-08-29, a
# security release). The tag moves with every 1.27.x patch; everything below
# was researched against the v1.27.3 source tree.
#
# Source: the v1.27.3 source tree, both configuration mechanisms, not a
# blanket os.Getenv grep (which would have missed almost all of this,
# because Gitea's real config surface is app.ini, not the environment):
#
#  1. `GITEA__<section>__<KEY>` -> app.ini. The image's
#     /usr/local/bin/environment-to-ini is a two-line wrapper for
#     `gitea config edit-ini --in-place --apply-env` (cmd/config.go), which
#     calls setting.EnvironmentToConfig (modules/setting/config_env.go).
#     Decoding: strip the `GITEA__` prefix, split section from key on the
#     first `__`, lowercase the section. A `.` in a section or key name is
#     written `_0x2E_` (`GITEA__git_0x2E_config__foo_0x2E_bar` sets
#     `[git.config] foo.bar`). A `__FILE` suffix means "read the value from
#     the file at this path" (trailing newline stripped). That is the
#     docker-secrets form, available for EVERY key, e.g.
#     GITEA__database__PASSWD__FILE=/run/secrets/db.
#     This pass runs on EVERY container start (docker/root/etc/s6/gitea/setup),
#     so these values always win over what is already in app.ini.
#     Caveat worth knowing: an empty value is still applied, writing an
#     EMPTY app.ini key, which is not the same as leaving the key out. Only
#     set a GITEA__ variable you actually want written.
#
#  2. Plain variables read by the image's shell entrypoint, not by Gitea:
#     docker/root/usr/bin/entrypoint (USER, USER_GID, USER_UID),
#     docker/root/etc/s6/gitea/setup (the envsubst pass over
#     /etc/templates/app.ini) and docker/root/etc/s6/openssh/setup (the
#     envsubst pass over /etc/templates/sshd_config). The app.ini template
#     pass is FIRST-BOOT ONLY: it runs solely when /data/gitea/conf/app.ini
#     does not exist yet, so on an existing data volume those variables do
#     nothing. The sshd_config pass runs on every boot.
#
#  3. A short list of GITEA_* variables the Go binary itself reads:
#     GITEA_WORK_DIR / GITEA_CUSTOM (modules/setting/path.go),
#     GITEA_RUN_MODE (modules/setting/setting.go:169),
#     GITEA_I_AM_BEING_UNSAFE_RUNNING_AS_ROOT (setting.go:192),
#     GITEA_RUNNER_REGISTRATION_TOKEN[_FILE] (services/actions/init.go).
#
# Defaults below are the ones in modules/setting/*.go at v1.27.3, not the
# ones in the docs. Where the two disagree, or the docs are silent:
#
#   * The docs' "Environment variables" page lists only GITEA_WORK_DIR,
#     GITEA_CUSTOM, USER, USERNAME, HOME and the Windows/Go runtime set. It
#     documents NONE of GITEA_RUN_MODE, GITEA_I_AM_BEING_UNSAFE_RUNNING_AS_ROOT,
#     GITEA_RUNNER_REGISTRATION_TOKEN, or any of the docker entrypoint
#     variables. Those come from the code and the image, and are grouped
#     under "Container runtime" and "First-boot app.ini template" below.
#   * GITEA_APP_INI and GITEA_TEMP exist ONLY in the rootless image
#     (Dockerfile.rootless). This template runs the ROOT image, whose s6
#     scripts hardcode ${GITEA_CUSTOM}/conf/app.ini; setting GITEA_APP_INI
#     here does nothing. Declared anyway, empty, so the difference is
#     visible rather than surprising.
#   * `[server] OFFLINE_MODE` no longer exists at 1.27; nothing in the tree
#     reads it. It is deliberately NOT declared.
#
# app.ini has several hundred settings across ~60 sections; enumerating them
# all here would be noise. This file covers what the compose sets plus the
# sections an operator actually touches on a self-hosted instance (server,
# database, security, service, mailer, actions, log). The complete surface is
# upstream-documented in the Configuration Cheat Sheet linked below. Any key
# on that page can be set here as GITEA__<section>__<KEY>.
#
# The compose prompts for ROOT_URL and POSTGRES_PASSWORD, which is all a
# fresh install needs. Nothing sets INSTALL_LOCK or SECRET_KEY, so the first
# visit to the site shows Gitea's install page, where the admin account is
# created; the database fields there are already filled in from the compose.

# @defaultSensitive=false @defaultRequired=infer
# @docs("Gitea Configuration Cheat Sheet", https://docs.gitea.com/administration/config-cheat-sheet)
# ---

# ─── Platform prompt keys ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The two values otterdeploy asks for before the stack is created. Each is
# threaded by the compose into the container variables that carry it.

# Full public URL of this Gitea, scheme included and trailing slash kept
# (`https` + `://` + the host you serve it on + `/`). It is what clone URLs,
# webhook payloads, avatar links and OAuth redirects are built from, so a
# wrong value produces a site that renders but hands out unusable URLs. The
# compose passes it as GITEA__server__ROOT_URL below. (The root image's
# first-boot app.ini template also reads a plain ROOT_URL, but the compose
# deliberately routes it through the GITEA__ form, which is re-applied on
# every restart.)
# @required
# @type=url
# @docs("server.ROOT_URL", https://docs.gitea.com/administration/config-cheat-sheet)
ROOT_URL=

# Password for the bundled PostgreSQL's `gitea` role. The compose puts the
# same value in the database container's POSTGRES_PASSWORD and in
# GITEA__database__PASSWD; the two must stay identical or Gitea cannot
# connect. Generate with `openssl rand -base64 24`.
# @required
# @sensitive
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# ─── Bundled PostgreSQL (service `db`) ─────────────────────────────────────
# Read by the postgres:16-alpine image's own entrypoint, only on the very
# first boot with an empty data directory, so changing them later does not
# rename anything. POSTGRES_PASSWORD is the prompt key declared above.

# Role the entrypoint creates and that Gitea logs in as. Mirrored by
# GITEA__database__USER.
# @docs("postgres image environment variables", https://hub.docker.com/_/postgres)
POSTGRES_USER=gitea

# Database the entrypoint creates. Mirrored by GITEA__database__NAME.
POSTGRES_DB=gitea

# ─── Database ([database]) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Which database driver to use. The compose runs PostgreSQL, so `postgres`.
# Leaving this at the image default (`sqlite3`, from the first-boot template)
# would silently ignore the bundled database.
# @required
# @type=enum(postgres, mysql, mssql, sqlite3)
# @docs("Database configuration", https://docs.gitea.com/administration/config-cheat-sheet)
GITEA__database__DB_TYPE=postgres

# Database endpoint as `host:port` (or an absolute path for a unix socket).
# Set by the compose to the bundled Postgres service. Platform-owned.
# @required
GITEA__database__HOST=${{stack.db.HOST}}:5432

# Database name. Must match the bundled Postgres's POSTGRES_DB.
# @required
GITEA__database__NAME=gitea

# Database role. Must match the bundled Postgres's POSTGRES_USER.
# @required
GITEA__database__USER=gitea

# Database password. Set by the compose from POSTGRES_PASSWORD; keep the two
# in sync. For a docker secret, use GITEA__database__PASSWD__FILE instead.
# @required
# @sensitive
GITEA__database__PASSWD=${POSTGRES_PASSWORD}

# libpq sslmode for the connection. Default `disable`, which is what the
# bundled Postgres speaks (plaintext on the private stack network). Only
# raise this for an external managed database.
# @type=enum(disable, require, verify-ca, verify-full)
GITEA__database__SSL_MODE=disable

# PostgreSQL schema to use inside NAME. Empty means the role's default
# search_path (normally `public`).
GITEA__database__SCHEMA=

# Log every SQL statement. Very noisy; for debugging a slow instance only.
# @type=boolean
GITEA__database__LOG_SQL=false

# Log a warning for any query slower than this. Duration string ("5s",
# "500ms").
GITEA__database__SLOW_QUERY_THRESHOLD=5s

# Connection pool sizing. MAX_OPEN_CONNS=0 means unlimited; raise
# MAX_IDLE_CONNS on a busy instance so connections are reused rather than
# reopened.
# @type=number
GITEA__database__MAX_OPEN_CONNS=0
# @type=number
GITEA__database__MAX_IDLE_CONNS=2
# Duration a pooled connection may live before it is recycled. `0` means
# forever; a proxy or managed database that closes idle connections needs a
# finite value here.
GITEA__database__CONN_MAX_LIFETIME=0

# Rows Gitea fetches per batch when iterating a large table (migrations,
# reindexing). Lower it if memory is tight.
# @type=number
GITEA__database__ITERATE_BUFFER_SIZE=50

# Run schema migrations automatically at startup. Turning this off means an
# upgrade will refuse to start until you migrate by hand. That is only for
# operators who deliberately gate migrations.
# @type=boolean
GITEA__database__AUTO_MIGRATION=true

# ─── Server & URLs ([server]) ──────────────────────────────────────────────

# The public URL, set by the compose from the ROOT_URL prompt key.
# @required
# @type=url
# @docs("Server configuration", https://docs.gitea.com/administration/config-cheat-sheet)
GITEA__server__ROOT_URL=${ROOT_URL}

# How Gitea itself serves: `http` (default) is correct behind otterdeploy's
# TLS-terminating proxy. `https` makes Gitea terminate TLS itself and then
# CERT_FILE/KEY_FILE are required.
# @type=enum(http, https, fcgi, http+unix, fcgi+unix)
GITEA__server__PROTOCOL=http

# Bare hostname (no scheme, no port) used for SSH clone URLs and as the base
# for the default NO_REPLY_ADDRESS. Usually the host part of ROOT_URL.
GITEA__server__DOMAIN=localhost

# Address the HTTP server binds to inside the container. `0.0.0.0` is what
# the container network needs; narrowing it will make the published port
# unreachable.
GITEA__server__HTTP_ADDR=0.0.0.0

# Port the HTTP server listens on. The compose exposes container port 3000
# with no override, so this is that port's twin: change one without the
# other and routing breaks.
# @type=port
GITEA__server__HTTP_PORT=3000

# TLS certificate and key paths inside the container, used only when
# PROTOCOL=https. Mount them into the container first.
# @docs("HTTPS Setup", https://docs.gitea.com/administration/https-setup)
GITEA__server__CERT_FILE=
GITEA__server__KEY_FILE=

# Prefix static assets are served from, for putting JS/CSS on a CDN. Empty
# means "serve them from ROOT_URL's sub-path", which is what you want unless
# you have a CDN.
GITEA__server__STATIC_URL_PREFIX=

# What anonymous visitors see at `/`.
# @type=enum(home, explore, organizations, login)
GITEA__server__LANDING_PAGE=home

# Turn off Git over SSH entirely. The root image runs an OpenSSH daemon on
# container port 22 (see the SSH template variables below); this compose does
# not publish it, so SSH clones are unreachable until you expose that port.
# @type=boolean
GITEA__server__DISABLE_SSH=false

# Use Gitea's own built-in SSH server instead of the image's OpenSSH daemon.
# With the built-in server, SSH_LISTEN_PORT is a port inside the Gitea
# process and no sshd is involved.
# @type=boolean
GITEA__server__START_SSH_SERVER=false

# Hostname shown in SSH clone URLs. Empty falls back to DOMAIN, so set it
# only when SSH arrives on a different name than HTTP.
GITEA__server__SSH_DOMAIN=

# Port shown in SSH clone URLs (what users connect to from outside), and the
# port sshd/the built-in server actually listens on inside the container.
# They differ whenever a proxy or a published-port remap sits in between.
# @type=port
GITEA__server__SSH_PORT=22
# @type=port
GITEA__server__SSH_LISTEN_PORT=22

# Username in SSH clone URLs (`git@host:org/repo.git`). Empty uses the
# process's run user, which is `git` in this image.
GITEA__server__BUILTIN_SSH_SERVER_USER=

# Serve Git LFS from this instance. Off by default; large-file workflows
# need it on, and it stores objects under the data volume.
# @type=boolean
# @docs("Git LFS setup", https://docs.gitea.com/administration/git-lfs-setup)
GITEA__server__LFS_START_SERVER=false

# ─── Security ([security]) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

# `true` skips the web installer. This compose leaves it unset, so the first
# visit shows the install page and creates the admin account there. Set it to
# true only together with SECRET_KEY and a database that is already
# populated, otherwise you lock yourself out of an uninitialised instance.
# @type=boolean
# @docs("Security configuration", https://docs.gitea.com/administration/config-cheat-sheet)
GITEA__security__INSTALL_LOCK=

# Key used to encrypt secrets at rest (2FA secrets, mirror and OAuth
# credentials, package registry tokens). Generated by the installer on first
# run and written into app.ini; supply your own only if you are restoring an
# existing instance, and never change it afterwards. The old ciphertext
# becomes unreadable. Prefer SECRET_KEY_URI for a file-backed secret.
# @sensitive
GITEA__security__SECRET_KEY=

# Read SECRET_KEY from a URI instead of inline, e.g.
# `file:/run/secrets/gitea_secret_key`. Setting both this and SECRET_KEY is a
# fatal error at startup.
GITEA__security__SECRET_KEY_URI=

# JWT signing key for Gitea's internal API (the one hooks call back on).
# Generated on first run; regenerating it invalidates in-flight hook calls.
# INTERNAL_TOKEN_URI is the file-backed form, and setting both is fatal.
# @sensitive
GITEA__security__INTERNAL_TOKEN=
GITEA__security__INTERNAL_TOKEN_URI=

# Hash used for new passwords. Empty selects the build's default
# (pbkdf2-based). Values carry tuning parameters, e.g.
# `argon2$2$65536$8$50`; a wrong or unknown value is fatal at startup.
GITEA__security__PASSWORD_HASH_ALGO=

# Minimum length for locally-managed passwords.
# @type=number
GITEA__security__MIN_PASSWORD_LENGTH=8

# Character classes a password must contain, comma-separated
# (`lower,upper,digit,spec`). Empty means no class requirement.
GITEA__security__PASSWORD_COMPLEXITY=

# Check new passwords against HaveIBeenPwned's k-anonymity API. Makes an
# outbound request on every password set; fails closed if the API is
# unreachable.
# @type=boolean
GITEA__security__PASSWORD_CHECK_PWN=false

# Days a "remember me" cookie stays valid.
# @type=number
GITEA__security__LOGIN_REMEMBER_DAYS=31

# Block the repository-level "git hooks" editor. Default `true`: a git hook
# runs arbitrary code as the Gitea user, so only turn this off if every user
# who can administer a repository is already trusted with shell access.
# @type=boolean
GITEA__security__DISABLE_GIT_HOOKS=true

# Block webhook creation entirely (webhooks can be used to probe the
# internal network from the server's vantage point).
# @type=boolean
GITEA__security__DISABLE_WEBHOOKS=false

# Header carrying the authenticated username when
# `[service] ENABLE_REVERSE_PROXY_AUTHENTICATION` is on. Only meaningful when
# a proxy you control strips the header from client requests first;
# otherwise anyone can send it and become anyone.
# @docs("Reverse Proxies", https://docs.gitea.com/administration/reverse-proxies)
GITEA__security__REVERSE_PROXY_AUTHENTICATION_USER=X-WEBAUTH-USER
GITEA__security__REVERSE_PROXY_AUTHENTICATION_EMAIL=X-WEBAUTH-EMAIL

# Comma-separated CIDRs/IPs whose X-Forwarded-For Gitea will believe when
# recording client addresses.
GITEA__security__REVERSE_PROXY_TRUSTED_PROXIES=127.0.0.0/8,::1/128

# Require pushes to carry Gitea's own environment (set by its hooks), which
# blocks pushes made directly on the server's filesystem from being processed
# as if they came through Gitea.
# @type=boolean
GITEA__security__ONLY_ALLOW_PUSH_IF_GITEA_ENVIRONMENT_SET=true

# ─── Users, registration & sign-in ([service]) ─────────────────────────────

# Close self-service sign-up. The usual setting for a private instance: turn
# it on after creating the accounts you need, and invite via admin instead.
# @type=boolean
# @docs("Service configuration", https://docs.gitea.com/administration/config-cheat-sheet)
GITEA__service__DISABLE_REGISTRATION=false

# Require a login for every page, so nothing is public. Accepts a boolean, or
# the literal `expensive`, which leaves cheap pages public but blocks the
# costly anonymous endpoints (code search, archive downloads, blame). Any
# other non-boolean string is fatal at startup.
GITEA__service__REQUIRE_SIGNIN_VIEW=false

# Require new users to confirm their email before the account activates.
# Needs a working [mailer] below, or nobody can ever sign in.
# @type=boolean
GITEA__service__REGISTER_EMAIL_CONFIRM=false

# Hold new registrations for an administrator to approve. Ignored when
# REGISTER_EMAIL_CONFIRM is on, since email confirmation wins.
# @type=boolean
GITEA__service__REGISTER_MANUAL_CONFIRM=false

# Send notification email for issue/PR activity. Off by default even when
# the mailer is configured.
# @type=boolean
GITEA__service__ENABLE_NOTIFY_MAIL=false

# Allow HTTP Basic auth (username + password or token) against the API and
# git-over-HTTP. Turning it off leaves OAuth/session auth only, which breaks
# `git clone` over HTTPS with a token.
# @type=boolean
GITEA__service__ENABLE_BASIC_AUTHENTICATION=true

# Show a CAPTCHA on the registration form, and pick which one. `image` is
# the built-in one and needs no third-party keys; the others need their own
# sitekey/secret pair (GITEA__service__RECAPTCHA_SITEKEY,
# GITEA__service__CF_TURNSTILE_SECRET, and so on).
# @type=boolean
GITEA__service__ENABLE_CAPTCHA=false
# @type=enum(image, recaptcha, hcaptcha, mcaptcha, cfturnstile)
GITEA__service__CAPTCHA_TYPE=image

# Hide new users' email addresses by default.
# @type=boolean
GITEA__service__DEFAULT_KEEP_EMAIL_PRIVATE=false

# Let ordinary users create organizations.
# @type=boolean
GITEA__service__DEFAULT_ALLOW_CREATE_ORGANIZATION=true

# Visibility new accounts get. Empty uses the first entry of
# ALLOWED_USER_VISIBILITY_MODES, i.e. `public`.
# @type=enum(public, limited, private)
GITEA__service__DEFAULT_USER_VISIBILITY=

# Domain used to build the `noreply` address that hides a user's real email
# in commits. Empty derives it from [server] DOMAIN as `noreply.<domain>`.
GITEA__service__NO_REPLY_ADDRESS=

# Comma-separated glob list of email domains allowed (or blocked) at
# registration. Empty means no restriction.
GITEA__service__EMAIL_DOMAIN_ALLOWLIST=
GITEA__service__EMAIL_DOMAIN_BLOCKLIST=

# ─── Outgoing email ([mailer]) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Nothing here is set by the compose: an instance without a mailer still
# works, it just cannot send confirmations, password resets or notifications.

# Master switch. Every other key in this section is ignored while it is off.
# @type=boolean
# @docs("Email setup", https://docs.gitea.com/administration/email-setup)
GITEA__mailer__ENABLED=false

# Transport. Empty is inferred from SMTP_PORT (25 -> smtp, 465 -> smtps,
# 587 -> smtp+starttls, anything else -> smtps with a logged error), so set
# it explicitly. `sendmail` shells out to SENDMAIL_PATH instead of using
# SMTP; `dummy` logs the message and sends nothing, which is useful for
# testing the flow.
# @type=enum(smtp, smtps, smtp+starttls, smtp+unix, sendmail, dummy)
GITEA__mailer__PROTOCOL=

# SMTP host, and port. An empty port is inferred from PROTOCOL (smtp -> 25,
# smtps -> 465, smtp+starttls -> 587).
GITEA__mailer__SMTP_ADDR=
# @type=port
GITEA__mailer__SMTP_PORT=

# SMTP credentials. Most providers want the full email address as the
# username and an app-specific password.
GITEA__mailer__USER=
# @sensitive
GITEA__mailer__PASSWD=

# Envelope/header From. Accepts a bare address or `Name <addr>`. Empty falls
# back to USER, which is wrong for providers that require a verified sender.
GITEA__mailer__FROM=

# Hostname sent in the SMTP HELO/EHLO. Empty uses the container's hostname,
# which some strict relays reject.
GITEA__mailer__HELO_HOSTNAME=

# Accept any server certificate, including self-signed ones. Only for an
# internal relay you control; it disables the TLS trust check.
# @type=boolean
GITEA__mailer__FORCE_TRUST_SERVER_CERT=false

# Prefix prepended to every outgoing subject line, e.g. "[Gitea] ".
GITEA__mailer__SUBJECT_PREFIX=

# ─── Actions (built-in CI) ([actions]) ─────────────────────────────────────
# Gitea Actions is enabled by default at this version, but it does nothing
# until at least one act_runner is registered against the instance.

# @type=boolean
# @docs("Actions Overview", https://docs.gitea.com/usage/actions/overview)
GITEA__actions__ENABLED=true

# Where a bare `uses: owner/action@ref` is resolved from: `github` fetches
# from github.com, `self` resolves against this instance's own ROOT_URL.
# @type=enum(github, self)
GITEA__actions__DEFAULT_ACTIONS_URL=github

# Days workflow logs and uploaded artifacts are kept before cleanup.
# @type=number
GITEA__actions__LOG_RETENTION_DAYS=365
# @type=number
GITEA__actions__ARTIFACT_RETENTION_DAYS=90

# Pre-seeds the global runner registration token so a runner can register
# without a human copying one out of the admin UI. Must be at least 32
# characters, and Gitea refuses to start if both this and its _FILE twin are
# set. Once a newer token has been generated in the UI this one is rejected.
# @sensitive
# @type=string(minLength=32)
# @docs("Gitea Runner registration", https://docs.gitea.com/usage/actions/act-runner)
GITEA_RUNNER_REGISTRATION_TOKEN=
GITEA_RUNNER_REGISTRATION_TOKEN_FILE=

# ─── Logging ([log]) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Comma-separated list of log outputs. `console` is what a container wants,
# putting the log on stdout where `docker logs` and otterdeploy's log view
# can see it. `file` writes into the data volume instead and the container
# then looks silent.
# @docs("Logging Configuration", https://docs.gitea.com/administration/logging-config)
GITEA__log__MODE=console

# @type=enum(trace, debug, info, warn, error, critical, fatal, none)
GITEA__log__LEVEL=info

# Directory for file-mode logs, inside the container.
GITEA__log__ROOT_PATH=/data/gitea/log

# ─── Container runtime ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Plain variables read by the image's entrypoint and by the Go binary, NOT
# mapped into app.ini. Baked-in values shown as defaults come from the
# Dockerfile.

# Numeric uid/gid the entrypoint rewrites the in-container `git` user to
# before starting Gitea. The reason to set them is a bind-mounted /data whose
# files are owned by a specific host user; with the named volume this compose
# uses, leave them empty.
# @type=number
# @docs("Installation with Docker", https://docs.gitea.com/installation/install-with-docker)
USER_UID=
# @type=number
USER_GID=

# Account name Gitea runs as inside the container; the image sets `git` and
# the entrypoint renames the passwd entry if you change it. It also ends up
# in SSH clone URLs. Changing it on an existing volume leaves file ownership
# behind.
USER=git

# Custom/config directory. The image sets /data/gitea, which is where the
# data volume is mounted and where app.ini is written; the s6 setup script
# uses it to locate ${GITEA_CUSTOM}/conf/app.ini. Repointing it without
# moving the volume loses the configuration.
GITEA_CUSTOM=/data/gitea

# Absolute path of Gitea's working directory. Unset in the root image (it
# derives the work path from the binary location); the rootless image sets
# /var/lib/gitea. Must be absolute; a relative value is fatal at startup.
GITEA_WORK_DIR=

# ROOTLESS IMAGE ONLY (Dockerfile.rootless). This template runs the root
# image, whose s6 scripts hardcode ${GITEA_CUSTOM}/conf/app.ini, so setting
# these here changes nothing. Declared so the difference between the two
# images is visible.
GITEA_APP_INI=
GITEA_TEMP=

# Overrides the app.ini `RUN_MODE`, checked before the config file. Anything
# other than `dev` is treated as production, deliberately, so a typo cannot
# accidentally expose dev behaviour.
# @type=enum(prod, dev)
GITEA_RUN_MODE=

# Escape hatch that lets Gitea start as uid 0. Gitea refuses to run as root
# otherwise, and the message is not a warning you should silence. Use it
# only while debugging a broken volume permission.
# @type=boolean
GITEA_I_AM_BEING_UNSAFE_RUNNING_AS_ROOT=false

# ─── First-boot app.ini template ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Read by docker/root/etc/s6/gitea/setup and substituted into
# /etc/templates/app.ini, but ONLY when /data/gitea/conf/app.ini does not
# exist yet. On any restart with an existing data volume they are ignored,
# which is exactly why this compose uses the GITEA__ forms above instead.
# The values shown are the entrypoint's own fallbacks.

APP_NAME=Gitea: Git with a cup of tea
# @type=enum(prod, dev)
RUN_MODE=prod
DOMAIN=localhost
SSH_DOMAIN=localhost
# @type=port
HTTP_PORT=3000
# @type=boolean
DISABLE_SSH=false
# @type=port
SSH_PORT=22
# Defaults to SSH_PORT when empty.
# @type=port
SSH_LISTEN_PORT=
# @type=boolean
LFS_START_SERVER=false

# @type=enum(postgres, mysql, mssql, sqlite3)
DB_TYPE=sqlite3
DB_HOST=localhost:3306
DB_NAME=gitea
DB_USER=root
# @sensitive
DB_PASSWD=

# Setting SECRET_KEY here makes the setup script flip INSTALL_LOCK to true
# on its own, which is how you get a first boot with no install page. Both
# only take effect on that first boot.
# @sensitive
SECRET_KEY=
# @type=boolean
INSTALL_LOCK=false

# @type=boolean
DISABLE_REGISTRATION=false
# @type=boolean
REQUIRE_SIGNIN_VIEW=false

# ─── OpenSSH daemon (root image) ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Substituted into /etc/ssh/sshd_config by docker/root/etc/s6/openssh/setup
# on EVERY boot, unlike the app.ini template above. SSH_PORT and
# SSH_LISTEN_PORT (declared just above) are shared with it: sshd binds
# SSH_LISTEN_PORT, defaulting to SSH_PORT.

# Written as sshd's MaxStartups / MaxSessions when non-empty; empty leaves
# the line out and sshd uses its own defaults.
SSH_MAX_STARTUPS=
SSH_MAX_SESSIONS=

# Path to an extra sshd config file to `Include`, for options this template
# does not expose. Mount the file in first.
SSH_INCLUDE_FILE=

# sshd LogLevel.
# @type=enum(QUIET, FATAL, ERROR, INFO, VERBOSE, DEBUG, DEBUG1, DEBUG2, DEBUG3)
SSH_LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# Paths to host-key certificates signed by an SSH CA, written as
# `HostCertificate` lines. Empty is normal; the setup script auto-detects
# /data/ssh/ssh_host_*_key-cert.pub if you mount certificates there, and
# generates plain host keys on first boot either way.
SSH_ED25519_CERT=
SSH_RSA_CERT=
SSH_ECDSA_CERT=
